[{"location":"bsdo/#Bath-Spectral-Density-Orthogonal","page":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","title":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"bsdo/#Theory","page":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","title":"Theory","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"bsdo/","page":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","title":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","text":"BSDO method uses Gauss quadrature to numerically integrate Eq. \\eqref{eq:fdt1}.  The unique feature of the BSDO method is that it uses the SD as the weight of a quadrature, enabling to construct an efficient set of polynomial interpolants.  In this method, we consider a more general frequency interval Omega_mathrmminOmega_mathrmmax.  Note that the choice of Omega_mathrmmin and Omega_mathrmmax affects the accuracy and convergence of the approximation, and it is desirable that the interval covers the entire frequency range of the QNSD i.e. S_beta(omega) should be small enough for omegaOmega_mathrmmin and omegaOmega_mathrmmax.","category":"page"},{"location":"bsdo/","page":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","title":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","text":"We first introduce the so-called hybridization function","category":"page"},{"location":"bsdo/","page":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","title":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","text":"    Lambda(z)=int_Omega_mathrmmin^Omega_mathrmmax mathrmd omega fracS_beta(omega)z-omega quad z in mathbbC","category":"page"},{"location":"bsdo/","page":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","title":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","text":"By the Sokhotski-Plemelj theorem, Eq. \\eqref{eq:lambda} implies","category":"page"},{"location":"bsdo/","page":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","title":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","text":"    S_beta(omega)=-frac1pi lim_etarightarrow 0^+operatornameIm Lambda(omega+i eta)","category":"page"},{"location":"bsdo/","page":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","title":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","text":"If Lambda(z) is approximated with a sum of rational functions as","category":"page"},{"location":"bsdo/","page":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","title":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","text":"    Lambda(z)approxsum_k=1^Mfracg_k^2(beta)z-omega_k","category":"page"},{"location":"bsdo/","page":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","title":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","text":"the BCF-QNSD relation can be expressed ","category":"page"},{"location":"bsdo/","page":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","title":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","text":"    beginaligned\n    C(t) \n    = -frac1pi operatornameImint_-infty^infty mathrmdomega lim_etarightarrow 0^+ Lambda(omega+ieta) mathrme^-iomega t\n    =sum_k=1^M g_k^2(beta)mathrme^-iomega_k t\n    endaligned","category":"page"},{"location":"bsdo/","page":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","title":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","text":"Thus we consider expressing Lambda(z) in the form of Eq. \\eqref{eq:lambda_disc} using a Gauss quadrature.","category":"page"},{"location":"bsdo/#bsdo_discr","page":"Bath Spectral Density Orthogonal","title":"bsdo_discr","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/#Basic-Theory","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/#Linear-Dissipation-in-Thermo-field-dynamics","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Linear Dissipation in Thermo-field dynamics","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/#Linear-dissipative-model","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Linear dissipative model","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"Let us consider a system linearly coupled to a bosonic heat bath with inverse temperature beta, which consists of a canonical distribution of harmonic oscillators. The Hamiltonian operator can be written as","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"H = H\\mathrm{S} + \\sumk \\omega{k} ak^\\dagger ak + V\\mathrm{SB}\\sum{k} \\frac{gk}{\\sqrt{2}} \\bigl(ak^\\dagger + ak\\bigr). $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"Here, H_mathrmS is the system Hamiltonian and V_mathrmSB is the system part of the system–bath coupling. The symbols a_k, a_k^dagger, omega_k, and g_k denote the annihilation operator, creation operator, frequency, and system–bath coupling constant for the kth mode of the bath, respectively.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"From a dynamical point of view, the effect of the bosonic heat bath at an inverse temperature beta on the system is exclusively determined by the bath correlation function (BCF) [Weiss-book]. One may write the BCF as","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"C(t)=\\frac{1}{\\pi} \\int{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\mathrm{d}\\omega \\; S\\beta(\\omega) e^{-i \\omega t}, $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"where the quantum noise spectral density (QNSD) is defined by","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"S_\\beta(\\omega)\\equiv \\frac{1}{2}J(\\omega)\\Bigl[\\coth\\Bigl(\\frac{\\beta \\omega}{2}\\Bigr)+1\\Bigr], $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"with","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"J(\\omega)=\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\sumk gk^2 \\Bigl(\\delta(\\omega-\\omegak)-\\delta(\\omega+\\omegak)\\Bigr). $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"In the following, we will refer to the above relation as the BCF-QNSD relation.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"Explicitly writing","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"\\begin{aligned} C(t) &= \\sumk \\frac{1}{2}\\Biggl{ \\frac{gk^2}{2}\\Bigl[\\coth\\Bigl(\\frac{\\beta \\omegak}{2}\\Bigr)+1\\Bigr] e^{-i\\omegak t} \\\n&\\quad\\quad - \\frac{gk^2}{2}\\Bigl[-\\coth\\Bigl(\\frac{\\beta \\omegak}{2}\\Bigr)+1\\Bigr] e^{i\\omega_k t} \\Biggr}, \\end{aligned} $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"we can state that when the BCF can be written in the above form, the corresponding dynamical system is described by the Hamiltonian given earlier and the bath is at the inverse temperature beta.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/#Thermo-field-dynamics","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Thermo field dynamics","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"The latter statement can be transformed into a powerful theoretical and computational tool by exploiting the Thermo Field Dynamics (TFD) framework. TFD is a methodology that facilitates the treatment of quantum systems at nonzero temperatures using the wavefunction formalism [UmezawaEtAl1982, Suzuki1991IJMPB, TakahashiUmezawa1996IJMPB]. Here we briefly review the TFD approach and demonstrate its relation to the above BCF-QNSD relation.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"We first consider the Hamiltonian of bosonic free particles","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"H\\mathrm{B} = \\sum{k}\\omegak ak^\\dagger a_k, $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"and label the eigenstates of the kth boson as ketn_k. Then, we introduce the so-called tilde space, denoted by kettilden_k, which is the Hilbert space of a fictitious dynamical system identical to the original physical system. Additionally, the tensor product of the physical and tilde spaces is referred to as the twin space [Suzuki1991IJMPB, Schmutz1978ZPB]. A ket vector in the twin space is given by","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"\\ket{mk,\\tilde{n}k} \\equiv \\ket{mk}\\otimes\\ket{\\tilde{n}k}, $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"from which the identity vector is defined as","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"\\ket{I} \\equiv \\bigotimesk \\sum{nk} \\ket{nk,\\tilde{n}_k}. $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"Using the canonical distribution operator","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"\\rho\\mathrm{eq}=\\frac{e^{-\\beta H\\mathrm{B}}}{\\mathrm{Tr}\\bigl(e^{-\\beta H_\\mathrm{B}}\\bigr)}, $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"and the definition of ketI, the so-called thermal vacuum state is derived as","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"\\begin{aligned} \\ket{0(\\beta)} &\\equiv \\rho\\mathrm{eq}^{1/2}\\ket{I} \\\n&=\\prodk \\Bigl[1-e^{-\\beta\\omegak}\\Bigr]^{1/2} \\exp\\Bigl(e^{-\\beta\\omegak/2}\\, ak^\\dagger\\tilde{a}k^\\dagger\\Bigr)\\ket{\\mathbf{0}} \\\n&= e^{-i G_\\theta}\\ket{\\mathbf{0}}, \\end{aligned} $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"where ketmathbf0=bigotimes_kket0_ktilde0_k represents the vacuum state of the ensemble of physical and tilde bosons, and","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"G\\theta=-i \\sumk \\thetak(\\beta)\\Bigl(ak \\tilde{a}k-ak^{\\dagger} \\tilde{a}k^{\\dagger}\\Bigr),\\quad \\thetak(\\beta)=\\operatorname{arctanh}\\Bigl(e^{-\\beta \\omega_k /2}\\Bigr). $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"The transformation e^-i G_theta is known as the thermal Bogoliubov transformation. It is worth noting that the transformation from the exponential form in the second line to the compact form in the third line can be performed using the Baker-Campbell-Hausdorff formulas for the mathrmsu(11) Lie algebra [Ban1993JOSABJ].","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"Let us now consider the case in which the initial density matrix of our model can be represented as the direct product of a pure state of the system, psi_mathrmeranglelanglepsi_mathrme, and the bath density matrix, i.e.,","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"\\rho(0)=\\frac{1}{Z} |\\psi\\mathrm{e}\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\mathrm{e}| \\, \\rho_{\\mathrm{B}}, $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"where Z is a properly defined partition function. Under this assumption, it is possible to demonstrate [BorrelliGelin2016JCP, BorrelliGelin2017SR, BorrelliGelin2021WCMS, GelinBorrelli2017AdP, deVegaBanuls2015PRA] that the expectation value of an arbitrary operator A acting on the Hilbert space of the physical system can be written as","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"\\langle A(t) \\rangle = \\langle A\\theta \\rangle{\\psi_\\theta(t)}, $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"where the wavefunction ketpsi_theta(t) satisfies the Schrödinger equation","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"\\begin{aligned} i \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\ket{\\psi\\theta(t)} &= H\\theta\\ket{\\psi\\theta(t)},\\[1mm] \\ket{\\psi\\theta(0)} &= \\ket{\\psi_\\mathrm{e}}\\otimes\\ket{\\mathbf{0}}, \\end{aligned} $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"with the thermal operators defined as","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"H\\theta=\\; e^{i G\\theta} \\hat{H}\\, e^{-i G\\theta},\\qquad A\\theta=\\; e^{i G\\theta} A\\, e^{-i G\\theta}. $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"The modified Hamiltonian operator hatH is defined by","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"\\hat{H}=H-\\tilde{H}_\\mathrm{B}, $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"where tildeH_mathrmB = sum_k omega_k tildea_k^dagger tildea_k is the free-boson Hamiltonian operator of the bosonic tilde space. The thermal Hamiltonian H_theta, which governs the finite-temperature dynamics, is obtained by applying the Bogoliubov thermal transformation to hatH:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"\\begin{aligned} H\\theta =\\; e^{iG\\theta} \\hat{H}\\, e^{-iG\\theta}  =&\\; H\\mathrm{S}+\\sumk \\omegak\\Bigl(ak^{\\dagger} ak-\\tilde{a}k^{\\dagger} \\tilde{a}k\\Bigr) \\\n&+\\; V\\mathrm{SB}\\sum{k} \\frac{g{k}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\Bigl{\\Bigl(a{k}+a{k}^{\\dagger}\\Bigr) \\cosh \\bigl(\\thetak\\bigr) \\\n&\\quad\\quad + \\Bigl(\\tilde{a}{k}+\\tilde{a}{k}^{\\dagger}\\Bigr) \\sinh\\bigl(\\theta_k\\bigr)\\Bigr}. \\end{aligned} $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"Representing the operators and frequencies of the physical and tilde spaces with common symbols as","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"a_ktildea_k rightarrow a_k\n,\nomega_k-omega_k rightarrow omega_k\n,\ng_kcoshtheta_kg_ksinhtheta_k rightarrow g_k(beta)\n,","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"the TFD Hamiltonian can be expressed in the simpler form","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"H\\theta =  H\\mathrm{S}+\\sumk \\omegak ak^{\\dagger} ak +V\\mathrm{SB}\\sum{k} \\frac{gk(\\beta)}{\\sqrt{2}}\\bigl(a{k}+a_{k}^{\\dagger}\\bigr). $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/#Connection-between-TFD-and-the-BCF-QNSD-relation","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Connection between TFD and the BCF-QNSD relation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"The parameters g_k cosh (theta_k) and g_k sinh (theta_k) entering the thermal Hamiltonian H_theta govern the coupling of the subsystem with the physical and tilde bosonic degrees of freedom. The spectral density (SD) for the thermal system–bath Hamiltonian can be written as","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"J\\theta(\\omega) = Jp(\\omega) + J_t(\\omega), $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"where","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"Jp(\\omega) \\equiv \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\sumk \\Bigl(g{k} \\cosh \\bigl(\\thetak\\bigr)\\Bigr)^2 \\delta\\bigl(\\omega-\\omega_k\\bigr)\\, \\Theta(\\omega), $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"Jt(\\omega) \\equiv \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\sumk \\Bigl(g{k} \\sinh \\bigl(\\thetak\\bigr)\\Bigr)^2 \\delta\\bigl(\\omega+\\omega_k\\bigr)\\, \\Theta(-\\omega), $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"and Theta(omega) is the Heaviside step function. These functions describe the system–bath couplings in the physical (subscript p) and tilde (subscript t) subspaces, respectively. As the temperature approaches zero, $Jp(\\omega) \\rightarrow J(\\omega)$ and Jt(\\omega) \\rightarrow 0$.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"By using the relations","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"\\cosh^2(\\thetak) = \\frac{1}{2}\\Bigl[\\coth\\Bigl(\\frac{\\beta\\omegak}{2}\\Bigr)+1\\Bigr], $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"\\sinh^2(\\thetak) = -\\frac{1}{2}\\Bigl[-\\coth\\Bigl(\\frac{\\beta\\omegak}{2}\\Bigr)+1\\Bigr], $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"one immediately sees that the definitions of S_beta(omega) and J_theta(omega) are equivalent, namely","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"J\\theta(\\omega) = S\\beta(\\omega). $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"Therefore, we have demonstrated that the system–bath Hamiltonian at temperature beta and the extended zero-temperature Hamiltonian have the same correlation function and hence the same dynamical behavior.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"In conclusion, whenever the BCF can be written in the form","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"$","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"C(t) = \\sumk \\frac{1}{2} gk^2(\\beta) e^{-i\\omegak t},\\quad \\omegak,\\,g_k(\\beta)\\in\\mathbb{R}, $","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Basic Theory","title":"Basic Theory","text":"the system dynamics at temperature beta is described by the TFD Hamiltonian given above. Note that in the above equation the frequencies omega_k can be negative. Unlike the standard TFD formalism [deVegaBanuls2015PRA, BorrelliGelin2016JCP], we do not perform thermo-field doubling but instead directly determine a set of temperature-dependent parameters (omega_k g_k(beta)).","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"CurrentModule = QFiND","category":"page"},{"location":"#QFiND","page":"Home","title":"QFiND","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Documentation for QFiND.","category":"page"},{"location":"#Index","page":"Home","title":"Index","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Basic Theory\nBath Spectral Density Orthogonal","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Modules = [QFiND]","category":"page"},{"location":"#QFiND.AAAfittedSD-Tuple{Float64}","page":"Home","title":"QFiND.AAAfittedSD","text":"(specdens::AAAfittedSD)(ω::Float64; scale::Float64=1.0) -> Float64\n\nCompute the spectral density for the Brownian oscillator model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#QFiND.BrownianSD-Tuple{Float64}","page":"Home","title":"QFiND.BrownianSD","text":"(specdens::BrownianSD)(ω::Float64; scale::Float64=1.0) -> Float64\n\nCompute the spectral density for the Brownian oscillator model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#QFiND.DrudeSD-Tuple{Float64}","page":"Home","title":"QFiND.DrudeSD","text":"(specdens::DrudeSD)(ω::Float64; scale::Float64=1.0) -> Float64\n\nCompute the spectral density for the Drude model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#QFiND.PowerLawExpSD-Tuple{Float64}","page":"Home","title":"QFiND.PowerLawExpSD","text":"(specdens::PowerLawExpSD)(ω::Float64; scale::Float64=1.0) -> Float64\n\nCompute the spectral density for the Power-law with exponential cutoff model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#QFiND.TannorMeyerSD-Tuple{Float64}","page":"Home","title":"QFiND.TannorMeyerSD","text":"(specdens::TannorMeyerSD)(ω::Float64; scale::Float64=1.0) -> Float64\n\nCompute the spectral density for the Tannor-Meyer model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#QFiND.bsdo_discr-Tuple{Function, AbstractVector{Float64}, Int64}","page":"Home","title":"QFiND.bsdo_discr","text":"bsdo(sbeta, ω::AbstractVector{Float64}, M_sp::Int)\n\nCompute the discretization of the QNSD using the BSDO method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#QFiND.evaluate_error-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}, AbstractVector{<:Number}, AbstractVector{<:Number}}","page":"Home","title":"QFiND.evaluate_error","text":"Calculate the error between the approximated and exact correlation functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#QFiND.id_freq-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{ComplexF64}, Int64, Bool}","page":"Home","title":"QFiND.id_freq","text":"id_freq_rank(f::AbstractMatrix{ComplexF64}, frank::Int, rnd::Bool)\n\nPerform interpolative decomposition on matrix f with a specified rank frank.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#QFiND.id_freq-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{ComplexF64}, Real, Bool}","page":"Home","title":"QFiND.id_freq","text":"id_freq_eps(f::AbstractMatrix{Float64}, eps::Real, rnd::Bool)\n\nPerform interpolative decomposition on matrix f with error tolerance eps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#QFiND.id_freq-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{Float64}, Int64, Bool}","page":"Home","title":"QFiND.id_freq","text":"id_freq_rank(f::AbstractMatrix{Float64}, frank::Int, rnd::Bool)\n\nPerform interpolative decomposition on matrix f with a specified rank frank.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#QFiND.id_freq-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{Float64}, Real, Bool}","page":"Home","title":"QFiND.id_freq","text":"id_freq_eps(f::AbstractMatrix{Float64}, eps::Real, rnd::Bool)\n\nPerform interpolative decomposition on matrix f with error tolerance eps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#QFiND.id_time-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{Float64}, Int64, Bool}","page":"Home","title":"QFiND.id_time","text":"id_time(f::AbstractMatrix{ComplexF64}, frank::Int, rnd::Bool)\n\nPerform interpolative decomposition on matrix f with a specified rank frank.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#QFiND.nnls_weight-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Complex}, Vector{<:Real}, AbstractMatrix{<:Real}}","page":"Home","title":"QFiND.nnls_weight","text":"nnls_weight(t, B)\n\nEstimate the coefficients (amplitudes) by solving a nonnegative least‐squares (NNLS) problem. \n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#QFiND.orthpoly_discretization-Tuple{Matrix{Float64}, Int64}","page":"Home","title":"QFiND.orthpoly_discretization","text":"orthpoly_discretization(wj, N)\n\nCompute the discretization of orthogonal polynomials.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"}]
